----------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
----------------------------------------------------------------------

mochallama links llama.cpp (and the ggml sources vendored inside it).
The llama.cpp tree is NOT checked into this repository — it is fetched at
build time into core/src/main/native/llama.cpp/, which is gitignored. By
default the build downloads llama.cpp's official prebuilt release
binaries; -Pnative=source builds them from that fetched tree instead.

The release is pinned by `llamaCppTag` in core/build.gradle (currently
b9371).

  - llama.cpp — https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp — MIT License
                Copyright (c) 2023-2026 The ggml authors

ggml is developed in its own repository (https://github.com/ggml-org/ggml)
but the copy mochallama uses is the one vendored inside the llama.cpp
tree, which carries no separate license file — llama.cpp's root LICENSE
covers both.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
What is actually distributed
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The `mochallama-core` jar contains no third-party code: native binaries
are excluded from it and published as separate `natives-<platform>`
classified jars.

Those `natives-<platform>` jars DO contain compiled llama.cpp/ggml code.
MIT requires its copyright and permission notice to accompany every copy
of the software, so each of those jars ships the upstream license text at:

  META-INF/licenses/LICENSE-llama.cpp

The same text is kept in-tree at core/src/main/native-licenses/ and is
packaged from there. When `llamaCppTag` is bumped, refresh that file from
the new tag.

Nothing in this NOTICE alters or supersedes the upstream license terms.
